# Flowy: build the node at a commit, in the same toolchain image its own Dockerfile compiles in.
#
# Every value here is transcribed from Dockerfile rather than chosen - @flowy-claude measured them
# so this file is a transcription and one decision:
#
#   Dockerfile:32  FROM golang:1.24-alpine AS build    -> SUT_BUILD_IMAGE
#   Dockerfile:49  CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -mod=vendor
#                  -trimpath -o /out/flowy .           -> SUT_BUILD_CMD, SUT_BUILD_ENV
#   Dockerfile:88  ENTRYPOINT ["flowy"]                -> SUT_BIN_NAME
#
# Not a shell program - build-sut.sh sources it, and anything the caller already exported wins, so a
# one-off can run against a different toolchain image without editing this file.

SUT_SRC_REPO=/home/dead/Projects/flowy

# THE ONE DECISION: THIS BUILDS THE NODE WITHOUT THE CONSOLE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#
# The image build is two stages - node:24-alpine builds web/dist, then the go stage COPYs it in -
# and build-sut.sh runs ONE image with ONE command. So a runner entry either reconstructs a
# two-toolchain image or drops a stage, and the honest answer is which stage a repro needs.
#
# It needs the go stage. web/dist/.gitkeep is committed exactly so that `//go:embed all:web/dist`
# succeeds on a tree where the console has not been built (console.go:18 says so), and a repro
# drives the API. Measured on 021a216 with the argv below: the build succeeds, the binary is 16.9MB
# and `flowy version` answers.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS: the console is not in this binary - it answers "not built into this binary"
# rather than serving a page. A repro whose subject is the CONSOLE cannot use this entry and needs
# the two-stage path (deploy/bootstrap.sh, or docker build). Say that in the row rather than
# discovering it from a blank page.
SUT_BUILD_IMAGE=golang:1.24-alpine
SUT_BUILD_ENV="CGO_ENABLED=0"
# -o flowy rather than -o /out/flowy: SUT_ARTIFACT is read relative to the source tree, which is
# what build-sut.sh copies out of and then removes.
SUT_BUILD_CMD="go build -mod=vendor -trimpath -o flowy ."
SUT_ARTIFACT=flowy
SUT_BIN_NAME=flowy

# NO BUILD STAMP, and this is worth knowing before somebody reads a version string and doubts it.
# The Dockerfile passes -X main.buildStamp=<sha>; a static env file has no sha to pass, so a binary
# built through here reports "0.8.0+src". The commit it came from is the one the runner asked for -
# the cache path carries it - not anything the binary will tell you.

# Vendored, so no network is needed and no dependency can differ from what the gate ran against.
SUT_REQUIRE_PATHS="vendor"
